<section id="section-1" class="logo-guide-section">
  <p class="guide-eyebrow">Foundation</p>
  <h2>Know what AI can and cannot do in logo design</h2>
  <p>
    AI can make ideas visible quickly. It can help a team compare visual territories, produce rough variations, test compositions, and place a concept into mockups. That makes it useful for exploration and iteration.
  </p>
  <p>
    AI cannot decide what the business should stand for, verify that a concept is distinctive in the market, guarantee accurate or editable typography, prove that a file is production-ready, or give a legal clearance opinion. A generated image is evidence that a direction can be pictured. It is not evidence that the direction should be launched.
  </p>
  <div class="boundary-grid" role="list" aria-label="Responsibility boundaries">
    <article role="listitem"><span>AI helps with</span><h3>Range and iteration</h3><p>Rough concepts, visual references, controlled variations, mockups, and repetitive exploration.</p></article>
    <article role="listitem"><span>People own</span><h3>Judgment and craft</h3><p>Positioning, creative direction, selection, geometry, typography, accessibility, and production quality.</p></article>
    <article role="listitem"><span>The business owns</span><h3>Truth and approval</h3><p>The offer, audience, real differentiators, risk tolerance, clearance decisions, and final sign-off.</p></article>
  </div>
  <aside class="guide-note"><strong>The real cost appears later.</strong> A generic or fragile mark becomes expensive after signs are printed, templates are built, applications are filed, and a team has learned to use it. Resolve uncertainty while the work is still easy to change.</aside>
</section>

<section id="section-2" class="logo-guide-section">
  <p class="guide-eyebrow">Step 1</p>
  <h2>Write the business brief before opening a generator</h2>
  <p>
    The first prompt should not ask for a “modern, professional logo.” Those words describe almost every generic result. Start with the decisions the identity must carry and the conditions it must survive.
  </p>
  <div class="brief-grid">
    <article><h3>Business truth</h3><ul><li>Specific audience and buying situation</li><li>Offer and category</li><li>Real reason customers choose the business</li><li>Geographic and language scope</li></ul></article>
    <article><h3>Visual direction</h3><ul><li>Three desired traits</li><li>Three traits to avoid</li><li>Category conventions to keep or reject</li><li>Prohibited imagery, symbols, and references</li></ul></article>
    <article><h3>Practical constraints</h3><ul><li>Favicon, mobile, social, documents, packaging, vehicles, or signs</li><li>One-color and reversed use</li><li>Print, embroidery, engraving, or vinyl limits</li><li>Longest name, descriptor, or multilingual version</li></ul></article>
    <article><h3>Decision ownership</h3><ul><li>Who narrows the shortlist</li><li>Who approves the business position</li><li>Who cleans the production files</li><li>Who owns clearance and final approval</li></ul></article>
  </div>
  <h3>Run a rights-aware preflight</h3>
  <p>
    Search the proposed business name before investing in a detailed mark. Exclude third-party logos, protected characters, and references you do not have permission to use. Record the source and license of fonts, icons, templates, stock elements, and uploaded images. Keep the original brief, generation history, shortlisted outputs, and notes about human redrawing.
  </p>
</section>

<section id="section-3" class="logo-guide-section">
  <p class="guide-eyebrow">Step 2</p>
  <h2>Choose tools by job, not popularity</h2>
  <p>
    Different tools solve different parts of the process. A guided logo maker can assemble quick starting options. An image generator can open wider visual territory. A collaborative design tool can organize review and applications. A vector editor is where geometry and production files are finished.
  </p>
  <aside class="source-note">
    <strong>Capability is not quality proof.</strong> The descriptions below summarize capabilities stated in official vendor documentation. They do not independently prove that one product makes better, more original, or legally available logos.
  </aside>
  <div class="table-shell tool-table">
    <table>
      <caption>Tool choice by stage</caption>
      <thead><tr><th>Job</th><th>Examples</th><th>Useful for</th><th>Watch for</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {toolRows.map((row) => (
          <tr><th scope="row" data-label="Job">{row[0]}</th><td data-label="Examples"><a href={row[4]}>{row[1]}</a></td><td data-label="Useful for">{row[2]}</td><td data-label="Watch for">{row[3]}</td></tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    Check the terms that apply to the exact account, plan, model, inputs, and intended use before the final commercial generation or download. Product features and terms change. No design tool replaces a clearance process.
  </p>
</section>

<section id="section-4" class="logo-guide-section">
  <p class="guide-eyebrow">Step 3</p>
  <h2>Use a prompt structure that produces useful differences</h2>
  <p>
    Hold the business facts constant. Ask for three visual territories that differ in one major idea, not thirty variations of the same badge. Start in black and white so color and presentation effects do not hide a weak silhouette.
  </p>
  <div class="brief-card" aria-labelledby="prompt-framework-heading">
    <p class="brief-label">Copy-and-use framework</p>
    <h3 id="prompt-framework-heading">A decision-ready logo prompt</h3>
    <pre><code>Create three clearly different logo territories for [hypothetical or real business name].

Business: [category, offer, and real difference]
Audience: [specific customer and buying situation]
The identity should feel: [three to five precise traits]
It must not feel: [three to five unwanted traits]
Required applications: [five to seven real uses]
Production constraints: [one color, embroidery, sign, small screen, and so on]

Explore:
A. A typographic territory tied to [business reason]
B. An abstract-symbol territory tied to [business reason]
C. A shape or pattern system tied to [business reason]

For each territory, show horizontal and compact versions in black and white. Explain the central idea, type category, weakest small-size detail, likely category-confusion risk, and one manual cleanup step.

Do not imitate an existing logo or identifiable artist. Do not use [prohibited category clichés]. Do not claim uniqueness, trademark availability, or production readiness.</code></pre>
  </div>
  <h3>Revise one variable at a time</h3>
  <p>
    A useful revision sounds like this: “Keep the symbol, composition, type category, and black-and-white palette unchanged. Remove the smallest internal turn and show three versions that differ only in the remaining line weight.” If color, type, icon, spacing, and mood all change together, the team cannot tell which decision helped.
  </p>
</section>

<section id="section-5" class="logo-guide-section">
  <p class="guide-eyebrow">Applied examples</p>
  <h2>Five practical hypothetical AI logo prompts</h2>
  <aside class="hypothetical-note"><strong>Important:</strong> Every business and name in this guide is hypothetical. The names have not been cleared for commercial or trademark use. The examples are educational, not client work or evidence of real results.</aside>
  <div class="prompt-stack">
    <article>
      <p class="prompt-number">01 / Local service</p><h3>Northline Mobile Vet</h3>
      <pre><code>Create three logo territories for the hypothetical business Northline Mobile Vet, which provides scheduled veterinary visits at customers’ homes for busy urban pet owners. The benefit is a calmer appointment with less travel and waiting-room stress. The identity should feel calm, capable, warm, organized, and contemporary, but not childish, clinical, luxurious, or sentimental.

It must work as a 24-pixel favicon, narrow mobile header, square social image, vehicle-door graphic, one-color folder mark, and embroidery. Explore: A) a restrained wordmark with a subtle directional detail in the N, B) an abstract route or doorway suggesting care arriving at home, and C) a compact path-and-shelter symbol. Do not use paws, hearts, crosses, animal heads, or house clichés. Show black-and-white horizontal and compact versions, rationale, small-size risk, and one manual cleanup step. Do not claim uniqueness or trademark availability.</code></pre>
    </article>
    <article>
      <p class="prompt-number">02 / B2B software</p><h3>VelaGrid</h3>
      <pre><code>Develop three logo territories for the hypothetical B2B software company VelaGrid. It helps field-service companies organize crews, equipment, work orders, and schedule changes around the daily dispatch board. The audience is operations directors and service-business owners who value clarity, reliability, and quick decisions. The identity should feel structured, responsive, precise, practical, and established, but not futuristic, playful, financial, or like cryptocurrency.

Test a product header, 16-pixel favicon, app icon, proposal cover, conference booth, and monochrome watermark. Explore: A) a custom wordmark using spacing to suggest coordinated movement, B) a modular grid with one clear route, and C) a compact monogram. Use no globes, rockets, lightning bolts, brains, circuits, shields, graphs, or infinity symbols. For each black-and-white direction, explain the rationale, confusion risk, 16/24/48-pixel tests, and one simplification.</code></pre>
    </article>
    <article>
      <p class="prompt-number">03 / Food and beverage</p><h3>Tide &amp; Timber Coffee</h3>
      <pre><code>Explore three original logo directions for the hypothetical neighborhood roaster Tide &amp; Timber Coffee. It sells through one coastal shop, local grocery partners, and recyclable mail-order packaging. The audience cares about freshness and local character without wanting technical jargon or luxury positioning. The identity should feel grounded, coastal, tactile, welcoming, and quietly distinctive, but not nautical-theme-park, rustic-western, tropical, or elite.

It must work on a two-inch bag label, one-color rubber stamp, storefront sign, circular avatar, invoice, and embroidered cap. Explore: A) a sturdy condensed wordmark, B) an abstract meeting of tide lines and timber grain, and C) a simple container shape with coast-and-craft negative space. Do not draw a cup, bean, anchor, wave, tree, or mountain. Avoid vintage badges, distress, scripts, and detailed illustrations. Show the mark alone, the wordmark, packaging risk, and manual redraw need.</code></pre>
    </article>
    <article>
      <p class="prompt-number">04 / Repair business</p><h3>FieldPatch Outdoor Repair</h3>
      <pre><code>Create three territories for the hypothetical company FieldPatch Outdoor Repair. It repairs tents, backpacks, sleeping bags, and outdoor clothing so customers can keep using equipment they own. The audience includes hikers, families, guides, and outdoor clubs. The business difference is skilled repair and dependable turnaround, not selling new gear or making unsupported environmental claims. The identity should feel resourceful, durable, human, useful, and optimistic, but not militaristic, survivalist, precious, or corporate.

Test a woven label, small repair tag, workshop sign, social image, one-color shipping stamp, and simple website header. Explore: A) a wordmark with one controlled stitched connection, B) an abstract patch with one repair line, and C) two incomplete forms joined into one stable symbol. Do not use mountains, pines, tents, compasses, recycling arrows, globes, or shields. Use solid shapes only. Identify small-size and embroidery risks.</code></pre>
    </article>
    <article>
      <p class="prompt-number">05 / Education</p><h3>BrightStep Language School</h3>
      <pre><code>Develop three territories for the hypothetical BrightStep Language School, which offers small-group classes for adults who need practical conversation for work, travel, and daily life. The audience may feel self-conscious about returning to a classroom. The difference is usable situations and steady progress rather than exams or status. The identity should feel encouraging, clear, adult, social, and dependable, but not child-focused, institutional, academic, loud, or like a translation app.

Test classroom window signage, a 24-pixel favicon, mobile course cards, a social image, workbook covers, and certificates. Explore: A) a warm wordmark with a subtle forward baseline, B) two simple shapes suggesting conversation, and C) a modular progression symbol. Do not use stairs, arrows, flags, speech bubbles, globes, books, or graduation caps literally. Flag cultural or interpretation risks and specify what target-language learners should review.</code></pre>
    </article>
  </div>
</section>

<section id="section-6" class="logo-guide-section">
  <p class="guide-eyebrow">Step 4</p>
  <h2>Generate territories, not dozens of random logos</h2>
  <p>
    A territory is a coherent answer to the brief. It has an idea, a silhouette, a type direction, a reason it fits the business, and a known risk. The team can discuss it without relying on taste alone.
  </p>
  <div class="boundary-grid" role="list">
    <article role="listitem"><span>Territory A</span><h3>Typographic</h3><p>Find distinctiveness in the wordmark, spacing, rhythm, or one custom letter detail. Risk: style can replace substance.</p></article>
    <article role="listitem"><span>Territory B</span><h3>Abstract symbol</h3><p>Translate the business position into a simple visual relationship. Risk: the idea can drift into familiar category geometry.</p></article>
    <article role="listitem"><span>Territory C</span><h3>Shape or pattern</h3><p>Build a compact mark and a wider visual language from one repeatable form. Risk: the system can become decorative or too detailed.</p></article>
  </div>
  <p>
    Document each territory with one sentence of rationale, the strongest application, the weakest application, the most likely confusion risk, and the next controlled revision. Shortlist the territory before polishing every variation.
  </p>
</section>

<section id="section-7" class="logo-guide-section">
  <p class="guide-eyebrow">Step 5</p>
  <h2>Score concepts before approving a favorite</h2>
  <p>
    Use the same six criteria for every finalist: relevance, distinctiveness, simplicity, scalability, flexibility, and usability. Score each from 1 to 5 and keep the written reasoning. A 1 means the concept fails the criterion in its current form. A 3 means it can proceed only with a named correction. A 5 means it works across the tested conditions without a material concern.
  </p>
  <div class="table-shell">
    <table>
      <caption>Completed hypothetical scorecard: Northline Mobile Vet, route-doorway territory</caption>
      <thead><tr><th>Criterion</th><th>Score</th><th>Reason</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {scoreRows.map((row) => (
          <tr><th scope="row" data-label="Criterion">{row[0]}</th><td data-label="Score"><strong>{row[1]}</strong></td><td data-label="Reason">{row[2]}</td></tr>
        ))}
        <tr class="total-row"><th scope="row" data-label="Criterion">Total</th><td data-label="Score"><strong>25/30</strong></td><td data-label="Reason"><strong>Promising, but not ready for approval.</strong></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <aside class="guide-note"><strong>Decision: refine, do not approve.</strong> Remove the smallest internal turn, redraw the compact version for 16 to 24 pixels, and repeat the one-color test. Reject the direction if simplification makes it look like a generic location pin or house.</aside>
  <h3>Use automatic rejection conditions</h3>
  <ul class="check-list">
    <li>Reject a concept that is unreadable or unrecognizable in a required application.</li>
    <li>Reject generated wording that cannot be re-typeset cleanly.</li>
    <li>Reject a direction that depends on gradients, texture, or mockup lighting to make sense.</li>
    <li>Pause a direction when a search reveals a close visual or verbal conflict that has not been assessed.</li>
    <li>Restart when the concept cannot be made relevant without adding a long explanation.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>A numeric score does not establish objective quality or legal availability. It makes the decision and its unresolved risks visible.</p>
</section>

<section id="section-8" class="logo-guide-section">
  <p class="guide-eyebrow">Worked system</p>
  <h2>Build the identity around one business position</h2>
  <p>
    The logo should not live in isolation. This hypothetical system shows how every layer can support the same position without turning the logo into a repeated decoration.
  </p>
  <aside class="hypothetical-note"><strong>Hypothetical example:</strong> Northline Mobile Vet is invented for this guide and has not been trademark-cleared.</aside>
  <div class="table-shell identity-table">
    <table>
      <caption>Northline Mobile Vet identity system</caption>
      <thead><tr><th>Layer</th><th>Decision</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {identityRows.map((row) => (
          <tr><th scope="row" data-label="Layer">{row[0]}</th><td data-label="Decision">{row[1]}</td></tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <h3>Test the palette as combinations, not swatches</h3>
  <p>
    For this hypothetical palette, Ink on Warm White, Graphite on Warm White, and Ink on Sea Glass are strong reading combinations. Terracotta should be tested carefully before it carries small text. The <a href="https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG22/Understanding/contrast-minimum.html">W3C guidance for WCAG 2.2 text contrast</a> sets a 4.5:1 minimum for normal text and 3:1 for qualifying large text. Text in a logo or brand name is exempt from that specific criterion, but the exemption does not cover the surrounding website, buttons, navigation, documents, or diagrams. <a href="https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG22/Understanding/non-text-contrast.html">Meaningful graphics and necessary interface cues</a> generally need 3:1 against adjacent colors.
  </p>
  <p>
    Do not use the logotype exemption as a design goal. Prepare a higher-contrast mark for difficult backgrounds whenever practical, and test the complete application rather than one isolated color pair.
  </p>
</section>

<section id="section-9" class="logo-guide-section">
  <p class="guide-eyebrow">Step 6</p>
  <h2>Clean the geometry and typography</h2>
  <p>
    Generated lettering is a picture of type, not a dependable wordmark. Re-typeset the business name with a licensed font or draw it deliberately. Correct spelling, spacing, kerning, repeated letters, punctuation, and optical balance. Keep a live-type master with the font information, plus an outlined production copy for vendors that do not have the font.
  </p>
  <p>
    For symbols, redraw weak curves and corners. Remove tiny notches, false joins, unnecessary anchor points, hidden objects, clipping masks, and details that disappear. Automatic tracing can produce editable paths, but editable does not mean clean. Adobe’s <a href="https://helpx.adobe.com/illustrator/desktop/manage-objects/traces-mockups-symbols/image-trace-panel-options.html">Image Trace documentation</a> describes controls for paths, corners, noise, shapes, and expansion. Those controls help create a starting vector. A person still needs to inspect the geometry.
  </p>
  <aside class="guide-note"><strong>Inspect the SVG.</strong> An SVG file can contain an embedded raster image. Open the file in a vector editor and confirm that the mark is built from real paths. A file extension alone does not prove resolution independence.</aside>
  <h3>Separate screen and print files</h3>
  <p>
    Keep RGB files for screens and prepare CMYK or spot-color specifications for print when the production method requires them. Ask the printer, sign maker, embroiderer, or fabricator for minimum stroke, gap, color, and file requirements. Run a physical proof before a full production run.
  </p>
</section>

<section id="section-10" class="logo-guide-section">
  <p class="guide-eyebrow">Step 7</p>
  <h2>Test the identity in real use</h2>
  <p>
    Mockups can help a team understand context, but presentation effects can also hide a weak mark. Test plain, measurable conditions first. Use the real business name, likely content density, actual interface sizes, and the production method that will be used.
  </p>
  <div class="table-shell test-table">
    <table>
      <caption>Pass or fail tests before launch</caption>
      <thead><tr><th>Application</th><th>Test</th><th>Pass condition</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {testRows.map((row) => (
          <tr><th scope="row" data-label="Application">{row[0]}</th><td data-label="Test">{row[1]}</td><td data-label="Pass condition">{row[2]}</td></tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    Record failures instead of hiding them. If the primary mark fails at 16 pixels but a purpose-built compact mark passes, document the switch point. If embroidery cannot hold an internal gap, create an approved embroidery version. A controlled variant is better than letting every vendor repair the logo differently.
  </p>
</section>

<section id="section-11" class="logo-guide-section">
  <p class="guide-eyebrow">Rights and clearance</p>
  <h2>Review rights before launch, not after rollout</h2>
  <aside class="legal-note"><strong>General information only, not legal advice.</strong> Laws, registrations, contracts, and risk differ by jurisdiction. Use qualified counsel for a business-critical clearance opinion, filing strategy, dispute, or material launch.</aside>
  <h3>Trademark search is more than an exact-name check</h3>
  <p>
    A logo generator does not determine whether a name or design is available. Start with the <a href="https://www.uspto.gov/trademarks/search">USPTO trademark search resources</a>, then examine similar wording, spellings, sounds, meanings, symbols, and overall commercial impressions, along with related goods and services. The USPTO’s <a href="https://www.uspto.gov/trademarks/search/likelihood-confusion">likelihood-of-confusion guidance</a> explains why a different font or added symbol may not resolve a conflict.
  </p>
  <ul class="check-list">
    <li>Search the proposed name, close spellings, phonetic equivalents, and translated meanings where relevant.</li>
    <li>Review visually similar symbols and dominant design elements, not only exact matches.</li>
    <li>Compare the goods and services attached to potentially similar marks.</li>
    <li>Record dates, search terms, owners, classes, links, and unresolved questions.</li>
    <li>Do not call a concept “trademark-safe” because one search returned no exact match.</li>
  </ul>
  <h3>Copyrightability and contract ownership are different questions</h3>
  <p>
    The <a href="https://www.copyright.gov/ai/">U.S. Copyright Office’s AI initiative</a> includes its January 2025 report on copyrightability. The report explains that copyright protects human-authored expression, that wholly AI-generated material is not protected, and that AI-assisted work can include protectable human contributions depending on the facts. Prompts alone generally do not provide sufficient control under the systems considered in that report. Document human selection, arrangement, redrawing, type work, geometry, spacing, and system design rather than assuming a long prompt settles the question. Read the <a href="https://www.copyright.gov/ai/Copyright-and-Artificial-Intelligence-Part-2-Copyrightability-Report.pdf">full Copyrightability report</a> for the Office’s analysis.
  </p>
  <h3>Separate four questions before commercial use</h3>
  <ol class="number-list">
    <li>Does the vendor contract permit the account to use this output?</li>
    <li>Does the output include licensed third-party content with separate conditions?</li>
    <li>Is the human contribution legally protectable in the relevant jurisdiction?</li>
    <li>Could the output conflict with another party’s trademark, copyright, publicity, privacy, design, or contractual rights?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    A vendor statement that “you own the output” addresses only part of the first question. It does not prove exclusivity, copyrightability, trademark availability, or non-infringement. For example, <a href="https://www.canva.com/policies/ai-product-terms/">Canva’s AI Product Terms</a> say outputs may not be unique and that licensed content remains subject to its license. <a href="https://docs.midjourney.com/hc/en-us/articles/32083055291277-Terms-of-Service">Midjourney’s terms</a> make ownership subject to stated conditions and third-party rights, while describing public visibility and remixing by default. <a href="https://openai.com/policies/services-agreement/">OpenAI’s business terms</a> assign output to the customer to the extent permitted by law, require input rights and user evaluation, and note that output may not be unique. <a href="https://www.figma.com/legal/ai-terms/">Figma’s AI terms</a> require human review and relevant clearances.
  </p>
  <p>
    Confirm the licenses for fonts, icons, templates, stock assets, and reference images across desktop design, websites, applications, merchandise, and third-party production. Do not upload a reference unless the business has the right to use it for that purpose. Re-check current terms before the final commercial generation or download.
  </p>
</section>

<section id="section-12" class="logo-guide-section">
  <p class="guide-eyebrow">Production handoff</p>
  <h2>Package the final identity so another person can use it</h2>
  <div class="package-grid">
    <article><h3>Master files</h3><ul><li>Editable vector master with clean paths</li><li>Live-type master with documented fonts</li><li>Outlined-type production copy</li><li>RGB and print color specifications</li></ul></article>
    <article><h3>Export set</h3><ul><li>SVG, EPS, and print-ready PDF</li><li>Transparent PNG exports at useful scales</li><li>Favicon and app-icon sizes</li><li>Light, dark, one-color, horizontal, and compact versions</li></ul></article>
    <article><h3>Application files</h3><ul><li>Social-avatar template</li><li>Proposal or document template</li><li>Signage, packaging, vehicle, or embroidery proof as relevant</li><li>Approved color combinations</li></ul></article>
    <article><h3>Governance</h3><ul><li>One-page usage sheet</li><li>File-naming and version convention</li><li>License and source record</li><li>Approval date, known limitations, and responsible owner</li></ul></article>
  </div>
  <aside class="guide-note"><strong>Simple handoff test:</strong> Give the package to someone who was not in the project. They should be able to find the correct mark for a dark website header, a one-color print job, and a social avatar without asking the designer.</aside>
</section>

<section id="section-13" class="logo-guide-section">
